Packages in Java

-------------------------------

What is a package:
- a way to group related classes and interfaces into a namespace
- works like folders in a file system — organizes code and avoids naming conflicts
- package statement must be the FIRST line in a source file (before imports and class)
- controls access: package-private members are visible only within the same package

  example:
      package com.mycompany.utils;  // this file belongs to this package

      public class StringHelper { ... }

-------------------------------

Package Naming Convention:
- always lowercase — no exceptions
- use reverse domain name to guarantee global uniqueness:
      com.google.search
      org.apache.commons
      com.mycompany.projectname.module

- structure mirrors the folder structure on disk:
      com/mycompany/projectname/module/MyClass.java

- if a name component is a reserved Java keyword, add an underscore:
      com.mycompany.int_  // "int" is reserved, so use int_

-------------------------------

Using classes from other packages — 3 options:

1. Fully Qualified Class Name (no import needed):
      java.util.List<String> names = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
   — verbose, used when two packages have a class with the same name

2. Explicit Import (specific class):
      import java.util.List;
      import java.util.ArrayList;
   — imports only what you name, exact and clear

3. Wildcard Import (entire package):
      import java.util.*;
   — imports all classes from the package

-------------------------------

Explicit vs Wildcard Import:

Explicit (import java.util.List):
- makes it immediately clear which classes are used and where they come from
- no risk of naming conflicts
- preferred in most professional codebases and style guides

Wildcard (import java.util.*):
- shorter, less clutter at the top of the file
- risk of naming conflicts if two packages have a class with the same name:
      import java.util.*;
      import java.sql.*;
      // Date is in BOTH — compiler error, ambiguous
- no runtime performance difference — compiler resolves wildcards to specific
  classes before generating bytecode, so compiled output is identical

  conflict fix — add one explicit import on top of wildcard:
      import java.util.*;
      import java.sql.*;
      import java.util.Date; // resolves the ambiguity, util.Date wins

-------------------------------

Static Import:
- imports static members (fields/methods) so you can use them without class name
      import static java.lang.Math.PI;
      import static java.lang.Math.*;  // wildcard version

      double area = PI * r * r;  // instead of Math.PI * r * r

- use sparingly — overusing makes it unclear where a name comes from

-------------------------------

Key rules to remember:
- package statement — first line, one per file, applies to all types in the file
- import statements — after package, before class definition
- classes in the same package do NOT need to import each other
- java.lang package (String, Math, System, etc.) is imported automatically